## Part Two

Now, the same rules apply as before, except if removing a single level from an unsafe report would make it safe, the report instead counts as safe.

More of the above example's reports are now safe:

```
7 6 4 2 1: Safe without removing any level.
1 2 7 8 9: Unsafe regardless of which level is removed.
9 7 6 2 1: Unsafe regardless of which level is removed.
1 3 2 4 5: Safe by removing the second level, 3.
8 6 4 4 1: Safe by removing the third level, 4.
1 3 6 7 9: Safe without removing any level.
```

Thanks to the Problem Dampener, **4** reports are actually **safe**!

Update your analysis by handling situations where the Problem Dampener can remove a single level from unsafe reports. **How many reports are now safe**?

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

### Code

```elixir
defmodule PartTwo do
  import Parser
  
  def resolve(input) do
    IO.puts("--- Part Two ---")
    IO.puts("Result: #{run(input)}")
  end

  def run(input) do
    input
    |> Enum.map(fn row -> valid_with_one_removal?(row) end)
    |> Enum.count(& &1)  # count how many were true
  end

  def valid_with_one_removal?(list) do
    0..length(list) - 1
    |> Enum.map(fn i -> List.delete_at(list, i) end)
    |> Enum.concat([list])  # also check without removing anything
    |> Enum.any?(fn version ->
      (increasing?(version) or decreasing?(version)) and threes?(version)
    end)
  end

end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, PartTwo, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 14, ...>>, {:valid_with_one_removal?, 1}}
```

### Tests

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)

defmodule PartTwoTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true
  import PartTwo
  import Parser

  @input [
              [7, 6, 4, 2, 1],
              [1, 2, 7, 8, 9],
              [9, 7, 6, 2, 1],
              [1, 3, 2, 4, 5],
              [8, 6, 4, 4, 1],
              [1, 3, 6, 7, 9]
          ]
  @expected 4

  test "part two" do
    actual = run(@input)
    assert actual == @expected
  end

  test "part two - valid with one removal" do
    actual = valid_with_one_removal?([1,3,7,6])
    assert actual == true
  end

  test "part two - invalid with one removal" do
    actual = valid_with_one_removal?([7,3,7,6])
    assert actual == false
  end

  test "part two - all input - one removal" do
    actual =
      @input
      |> Enum.map(fn list -> valid_with_one_removal?(list) end)
    assert actual == [true, false, false, true, true, true]
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

